\optsec{3.2}{The cost system}
Let $w$ be the wage, $p$ the price of the final good, $p_m$ the rental of a machine-hour, and $V_m$ the price of a machine. Under constant returns and competitive free entry, each good's price equals its unit cost. Since $\gamma$ increases on the open tasks, a producer who compares the two inputs task by task gives machines, up to a set of measure zero, the open tasks below some threshold $x$ and labor the tasks above it. With machines on the tasks below $x$, a unit of the final good uses $J(x)=\int_0^x\gamma(t)\,dt$ machine-hours and $1-x$ hours, so
\optdisp{p=w(1-x)+p_mJ(x),\qquad V_m=ap_m+\lambda w+br,\qquad p_m=(\rho+\delta)V_m,}{(2)}
and space rents at $r$. The last equation is the condition of a competitive rental market for machine-hours. A machine rented out for a period loses $\delta V_m$ of its value, and its owner holds it only if it also earns the return $\rho V_m$, so a machine-hour rents at the user cost $(\rho+\delta)V_m$. Appendix A derives it from the owner's problem when the owner consumes and holds machines (Lemma A.1(c)); otherwise Definition 1 imposes it.

The machine rows are a recursion: a machine-hour's price includes the machine-hours used to build the machine, whose price includes those used to build them, and so on. Substituting the machine's price into its rental again and again gives
\optdisp{p_m=(\rho+\delta)(\lambda w+br)\bigl[1+(\rho+\delta)a+\bigl((\rho+\delta)a\bigr)^2+\cdots\bigr].}{}
Tracing these costs through successive rows reaches the human hours and non-produced services in the full chain. Since $(\rho+\delta)a<1$, the series converges, and
\optdisp{p_m=w\widetilde\lambda_m+r\widetilde b_m,\qquad
\widetilde\lambda_m=\frac{(\rho+\delta)\lambda}{1-(\rho+\delta)a},\qquad
\widetilde b_m=\frac{(\rho+\delta)b}{1-(\rho+\delta)a}.}{(3)}
We call the tracing of prices through intermediate inputs the \emph{cost recursion}. It resolves every price into hours and land. For the final good ($i=g$) and the machine-hour ($i=m$),
\optdisp{p_i=w\widetilde\lambda_i+r\widetilde b_i,\qquad
\widetilde\lambda_g=1-x+\widetilde\lambda_mJ(x),\qquad
\widetilde b_g=\widetilde b_mJ(x);}{(4)}
a machine has the coefficients of a machine-hour divided by $\rho+\delta$, and space has $\widetilde\lambda=0$ and $\widetilde b=1$. We call $\widetilde\lambda_i$ and $\widetilde b_i$ the \emph{price coefficients}: the full-chain hours and land services per unit of each good, valued at the user cost. Equations (2)--(4) are the input--output price equations of Leontief (1936) and Sraffa (1960) for four goods; Appendix A writes them as a matrix system.

\optsec{3.3}{Households}
\begin{optthm}{Assumption 2}{workers}
There are $N>0$ workers. Each has one indivisible hour per period, which it sells ($n=1$) or keeps ($n=0$), and consumes its income each period, neither saving nor borrowing. A worker who consumes $c_g$ units of the final good and $c_h$ units of space and sells $n$ hours has utility
\optdisp{\log\min\{c_g,\,c_h/h\}-\chi n,\qquad h>0.}{(5)}
The cost of work $\chi\ge0$ differs across workers, with a continuous distribution function $F$ and $F(0)=0$.
\end{optthm}
A worker therefore consumes baskets of one unit of the final good and $h$ units of space, each costing $P_s=p+hr$, the subscript standing for support. Each worker receives one basket per period as support, in work and in exit (Assumption 3), and spends its wage on additional baskets. It consumes $1+wn/P_s$ baskets, and the utility of the choice $n$ is
\optdisp{u_n=\log\left(1+\frac{wn}{P_s}\right)-\chi n.}{(6)}
Consumption is measured in baskets, with one supplied through support. The parameter $\chi$ is the utility cost of the market hour, including the value of time it displaces. Non-work time is valuable and heterogeneous (Mas and Pallais 2019). A worker's reservation wage $s$ is the payment that makes work as attractive as the best available arrangement without that hour's earnings. Section 4.2 derives it from support, living costs, and preferences.

\begin{optthm}{Assumption 3}{the owner and support}
One further household, the owner, does not work and lives forever. It owns the land and the machines, and it gives each worker one basket per period, in work and in exit. It ranks paths of its own consumption $c_{o,t}$, in baskets, by $\sum_{t\ge0}\beta^tu(c_{o,t})$, with $\beta\in(0,1]$ and $u$ concave and increasing; Appendix A gives the precise conditions and the case $\beta=1$.
\end{optthm}
Each period the owner rents out $\ell\le T$ units of land and its $X$ machines, provides the support, replaces the machines that wear out, and consumes. With a constant stock, the rentals $(\rho+\delta)V_mX$ of its machines pay for the $\delta X$ replacements and leave the return $\rho V_mX$, so its budget is
\optdisp{P_s\,(c_o+N)=r\ell+\rho V_mX.}{(7)}
Support is a commitment in this budget, and it is \emph{funded} when the budget leaves $c_o\ge0$. The owner is not among the $N$ workers, so ``per person'' below means per worker. Our participation benchmark focuses on dependence: a household or public institution provides the living standard available outside employment. If support continues during employment, earnings buy additional consumption. Here the support is funded from ownership income, the rent on land and the return on machines; Section 6 lets the government provide it from a tax on rents, and shows why support financed from wages faces an aggregate limit as wages fall relative to rents.

\optsec{3.4}{Equilibrium}
\begin{optthm}{Definition 1}{equilibrium}
A \emph{stationary equilibrium} consists of prices $w$, $r$, $p$, $p_m$, and $V_m$, all nonnegative, with $P_s>0$; a threshold $x^*\in[0,x_H]$, machines performing the tasks below it and labor the others; the output $Y$ of the final good and the machine stock $X$; each worker's choice $n(\chi)\in\{0,1\}$; and the land $\ell\in[0,T]$ that the owner rents out and its consumption $c_o\ge0$, such that:
(i) \emph{producers} minimize cost and earn zero profit: $p_m\gamma(x)\le w$ at the open tasks below $x^*$ and $p_m\gamma(x)\ge w$ at those above it, prices satisfy (2) at $x=x^*$, and machine-hours rent at the user cost;
(ii) \emph{each worker} chooses $n(\chi)$ to maximize (6);
(iii) \emph{the owner}'s plan of renting out $\ell$, holding $X$, and consuming $c_o$, given by (7), in every period is optimal for it at these prices, starting from the stock $X$;
(iv) \emph{markets clear} for labor, machine-hours, land, and the final good:
\optdisp{N_a=Y(1-x^*)+\lambda\delta X,\quad
X=YJ(x^*)+a\delta X,\quad
\ell=hY+b\delta X,\quad
Y=N+\frac{wN_a}{P_s}+c_o,}{}
where $N_a=N\int n(\chi)\,dF(\chi)$ is the number of workers who work.
\end{optthm}
The equilibrium concept is standard: a stationary competitive equilibrium, in which every agent optimizes at constant prices and every market clears. Appendix A states the owner's problem in condition (iii) and solves it (Lemma A.1): at the user cost, the constant plan is optimal whenever (7) leaves $c_o\ge0$, and at a positive rent the owner rents out all its land. When $r>0$ we measure prices in land services, $r=1$, and write $v=w/r$ for the wage--rent ratio.

We call the threshold the \emph{task-assignment margin}, or task margin.
\begin{optthm}{Definition 2}{margins}
In an equilibrium with $w>0$ and $p_m>0$, the margin is \emph{interior} if $0<x^*<x_H$. The assignment is a \emph{boundary assignment} if $x^*=x_H$, so that labor performs none of the open tasks, and a \emph{corner} if $x^*=0$, so that machines perform none.
\end{optthm}
An interior margin can coexist with human-required tasks in $H$. Appendix E treats the case in which human-required services determine the wage.

\begin{optthm}{Definition 3}{automation}
Compare two economies that differ only as stated. \emph{Task automation} raises machine capability: $\gamma_M$ is nowhere lower and is higher on a set of tasks of positive measure, with the reserved tasks unchanged, so that $\gamma$ is nowhere higher and some tasks may leave $H$. \emph{Recursive automation} lowers $\lambda$, the labor needed to build a machine.
\end{optthm}

\optsec{4}{Equilibrium}
Proofs of the main price and participation results are collected in Appendix A.

\optsec{4.1}{Prices}
Quantities use the replacement rate in place of the user cost: a stock of $X$ machines supplies $X$ machine-hours and needs $\delta X$ new machines each period.
\begin{optthm}{Lemma 1}{prices and quantities}
In every equilibrium with margin $x^*$:
(i) prices are (3)--(4) at $x=x^*$, and the basket costs $P_s=w\widetilde\lambda_s+r\widetilde b_s$, with $\widetilde\lambda_s=1-x^*+\widetilde\lambda_mJ(x^*)$ and $\widetilde b_s=h+\widetilde b_mJ(x^*)$;
(ii) $Y$ baskets employ $N_a=YL_s$ hours and use $\ell=YB_s$ units of land, with $L_s=1-x^*+\lambda\delta J(x^*)/(1-a\delta)$ and $B_s=h+b\delta J(x^*)/(1-a\delta)$;
(iii) $\widetilde\lambda_s\ge L_s$ and $\widetilde b_s\ge B_s$, with equality when $\rho=0$, and $P_s=wL_s+rB_s+\rho V_mX/Y$.
\end{optthm}
Part (i) applies (4) to the basket, part (ii) solves the clearing conditions of Definition 1, and part (iii) compares $(\rho+\delta)/[1-(\rho+\delta)a]$ with $\delta/(1-\delta a)$ (Appendix A). The coefficients $L_s$ and $B_s$ are the hours and land services that one basket physically requires; with a positive return, the basket's price also pays the return on the machines that supply it.

\begin{optthm}{Proposition 2}{replacement closure}
In every equilibrium with an interior margin and $r>0$, the feedback term $1-\gamma(x^*)\widetilde\lambda_m$ is positive and
\optdisp{\frac{p_m}{r}=\frac{\widetilde{b}_m}{1-\gamma(x^*)\widetilde{\lambda}_m},\qquad
\frac{w}{r}=\frac{\gamma(x^*)\widetilde{b}_m}{1-\gamma(x^*)\widetilde{\lambda}_m},}{(9)}
with $\widetilde\lambda_m$ and $\widetilde b_m$ given by (3). Holding the other price coefficients fixed, the wage--rent ratio rises with $\gamma(x^*)$ and with $\widetilde{\lambda}_m$.
\end{optthm}
Substituting $w=p_m\gamma(x^*)$ from (8) into the machine-hour's price (3) gives $p_m(1-\gamma(x^*)\widetilde\lambda_m)=r\widetilde b_m$, and solving gives (9); Appendix A gives the derivatives. The closure holds in any production network of Appendix B.1 whose machine chain uses a non-produced input (Proposition B.1).

The denominator measures the feedback from the wage into the cost of its substitute. At the margin, $\gamma(x^*)\widetilde\lambda_m=w\widetilde\lambda_m/p_m$ is the part of the machine-hour's price that resolves into wages, including the return on the wages advanced to build machines; when $\rho=0$ it is labor's share of machine-service cost. A positive rent bill requires it to be below one. The closure is a price condition at the equilibrium assignment; household choices and market clearing also determine $x^*$ (Theorem 1).

Holding the other terms in (9) fixed, either channel (Definition 3) lowers the wage supported by a given rent. With labor in the machine supply chain, task automation also cheapens that chain's labor. Figure 2 traces both channels from a common starting point.

When the labor requirement of the full machine-service chain vanishes and $\gamma(x^*)\widetilde{\lambda}_m\to0$, equation (9) gives $(p_m/r)/\widetilde b_m\to1$. When $\rho=0$, the service price is then accounted for by its embodied claim on the non-produced input.

\optsec{4.2}{Participation and the labor market}
\begin{optthm}{Proposition 3}{dependence, living costs, and participation}
In every equilibrium, work is optimal for a worker with cost of work $\chi$ exactly when
\optdisp{w \ge s,\qquad s = (e^{\chi}-1)P_s,}{(10)}
and employment is
\optdisp{N_a=NF\left(\log\left[1+\frac{w}{P_s}\right]\right).}{(11)}
For $\chi>0$, the reservation wage rises with the cost of the basket.
\end{optthm}
By (6), exit gives utility zero and work gives $\log(1+w/P_s)-\chi$; workers indifferent between the two have measure zero because $F$ is continuous (Appendix A).

At a fixed wage, a higher basket price reduces the consumption gained from working. With support maintained, participation weakly falls. The condition depends on the wage relative to living costs, so a proportional rescaling of prices leaves the choice unchanged.

Replacement pricing and participation jointly determine the wage and task assignment. If $r>0$, the owner rents out all its land, so by Lemma 1(ii) the economy produces $T/B_s$ baskets and employs $TL_s/B_s$ hours. With Proposition 3, labor-market clearing is
\optdisp{\underbrace{\frac{TL_s}{B_s}}_{\text{hours required}}
=\underbrace{NF\left(\log\left[1+\frac{w}{P_s}\right]\right)}_{\text{hours supplied}}.}{(12)}
The requirements $L_s$ and $B_s$ depend on task assignment. Together with replacement pricing in (9), this condition links participation to the equilibrium margin $x^*$. Section 4.3 gives conditions under which it has exactly one solution and that solution is an equilibrium.

\begin{optthm}{Theorem 1}{existence and uniqueness}
Suppose
\optdisp{T>Nh,\qquad N_S(x_c)>N_D(x_c).}{(15)}
Then every equilibrium has $w>0$, $r>0$, and an interior margin, at the unique $x^*\in(0,x_c)$ with $N_D(x^*)=N_S(x^*)$. Up to the choice of numeraire and sets of workers of measure zero, its prices are (4) and (13) at $x^*$, and its quantities are $Y=T/B_s(x^*)$, $X=YJ(x^*)/(1-a\delta)$ and $N_a=N_D(x^*)$. An equilibrium exists if and only if the owner's income covers the support there, $rT+\rho V_mX\ge NP_s$.
\end{optthm}
As the margin rises, labor demand falls and labor supply does not fall, so (15) leaves one crossing; the proof (Appendix A) uses (15) and the user cost to rule out every other configuration, and the owner's budget decides whether the crossing is an equilibrium. When the wall lies below the crossing, (15) fails, and labor works only in $H$ and in the machine chain; the theorem does not cover that case.

\begin{optthm}{Corollary 1}{labor supply and the margin}
(i) As the margin $x$ rises on $(0,x_c)$, labor demand $N_D(x)$ falls, while the wage--rent ratio $v(x)$ and the real wage $v(x)/P_s(x)$ rise.
(ii) If the distribution of work costs changes from $F$ to $\widehat F\le F$ at fixed technology and support, and (15) and funding hold under both, the equilibrium margin, wage--rent ratio, and real wage weakly rise, and strictly if supply falls at the original margin.
\end{optthm}
Part (i) differentiates (13) and (14) and does not need (15); Appendix A also gives the rate at which $v(x)$ rises, the rate of Proposition 1(ii) amplified because the machine rental rises with the margin. For part (ii), $\widehat F\le F$ lowers labor supply at every $x$, and with demand falling in $x$ the crossing cannot move down.

\optsec{5.1}{What an hour's wage buys}
For every good or service, the price coefficients in (4) give an exact price. A feasible alternative method also bounds that price from above. A worker can make a unit of the final good by hand in an hour, using neither machines nor land.

\begin{optthm}{Proposition 4}{the real-wage fork}
In every equilibrium with $w,r>0$, every good or service $i$ satisfies
\optdisp{p_i=w\widetilde\lambda_i+r\widetilde b_i,\qquad
\frac{w}{p_i}=\frac{1}{\widetilde\lambda_i+\widetilde b_i(r/w)}.}{(16)}
An hour's wage buys $w/r$ units of space and at least one unit of the final good; when machines perform some tasks, it buys at most $(w/r)/\widetilde b_g$ units of the final good, where $\widetilde b_g=\widetilde b_mJ(x^*)>0$.
\end{optthm}
Below $x^*$ machines cost no more than labor, so $p\le w$; every price coefficient is nonnegative, so $p\ge r\widetilde b_g$ (Appendix A). Combining the task margin of Acemoglu and Restrepo (2018) with the input--output price logic of Leontief (1936) and Sraffa (1960) yields these purchasing-power bounds. Caselli and Manning (2019) prove that real wages cannot fall in terms of goods produced entirely from reproducible inputs; Proposition 4 gives a lower bound from feasible human production and shows how persistent requirements for non-produced inputs constrain wage purchasing power.

In any production network (Appendix B.1), a good $i$ that people can make with a \emph{human chain}, which uses neither machines nor machine-hours anywhere and has full-chain requirements $\bar\lambda_i$ of hours and $\bar b_i$ of non-produced services, satisfies
\optdisp{\frac{1}{\bar\lambda_i+\bar b_i(r/w)}\le\frac{w}{p_i}\le\frac{w/r}{\widetilde b_i}}{(17)}
when $\widetilde b_i>0$ (Proposition B.1). Here the final good has $\bar\lambda=1$ and $\bar b=0$, and space, which rents at $r$, has $\widetilde b=1$.

The rent bound counts the whole supply chain. An hour's wage buys $w/r$ units of the non-produced service, and the price of a unit of output carries $\widetilde b_i$ units, the return on the machines in its chain included; when $\rho=0$, $\widetilde b_i$ is the input the unit embodies. A good with no direct requirement may still use that input through electricity, materials, or machinery. The human-method bound likewise counts its entire chain. For cloth, consider a feasible hand-production method covering the fibers, spinning, weaving, tools, and workspace. The wage and rent bill for that complete chain caps the competitive price of cloth. Cheaper machine production raises what an hour's wage can buy above the lower bound. Ownership income and transfers fund consumption in addition to wages. Appendix B.6 gives the task-level representation of these costs.

Changes in purchasing power depend jointly on the wage--rent ratio and the requirements embodied in each good. Building taller can supply more floor space on a given site, lowering direct site area per unit. Higher crop yields can reduce the acreage needed for a given number of calories. These changes also alter requirements for structures, machinery, fertilizer, and energy; $\widetilde b_i$ counts the non-produced inputs throughout those supply chains, valued at the user cost. At given wages, rents, and $\widetilde\lambda_i$, a lower $\widetilde b_i$ reduces the price. Equilibrium affordability also depends on the wage--rent ratio. The ratio $\bar b_i/\bar\lambda_i$ describes how strongly rents enter the human-method lower bound; ordering actual price changes requires the selected recipes.

\begin{optthm}{Corollary 2}{vanishing wage purchasing power and participation}
Along a sequence of equilibria with interior margins and a fixed $h$, suppose
\optdisp{\gamma(x^*)\widetilde b_m\to0,\qquad 1-\gamma(x^*)\widetilde\lambda_m\ge\xi>0.}{}
Then $w/r\to0$: an hour's wage buys ever less space, while it still buys at least one unit of the final good. If, in addition, the distribution $F$ of work costs is fixed, then
\optdisp{\frac{w}{P_s}\to0,\qquad \frac{N_a}{N}\to0.}{(18)}
\end{optthm}
By (9), $w/r\le\gamma(x^*)\widetilde b_m/\xi\to0$; since $P_s\ge rB_s\ge rh$, also $w/P_s\to0$, and (11) gives the limit of participation (Appendix A). Section 6.1 states the condition on public payments under which this limit holds with policy.

The conditions concern replacement at the equilibrium margin. At fixed positive $\gamma(x^*)$ and $\widetilde b_m$, eliminating embodied labor from machine services leaves $w/r=\gamma(x^*)\widetilde b_m>0$. Recursive automation alone therefore cannot drive the wage--rent ratio to zero: people leave work only as machines improve at the tasks people still do. Human-required work can sustain a boundary assignment, and falling non-produced input requirements can sustain purchasing power. Section 7 gives a complete interior equilibrium and a sequence with a persistent hundred-to-one capability ratio across tasks.

The participation conclusion depends on maintaining the outside option. As wages buy less additional consumption, fewer people choose work when their basic support remains available. If providers' resources shrink and support falls, the participation response can change. Section 6 connects the funded basket to the income available for redistribution.

\optsec{5.2}{Factor income while labor remains employed}
\begin{optthm}{Proposition 5}{labor's share}
In every equilibrium, final expenditure equals factor income,
\optdisp{I=wN_a+rT+\rho V_mX,}{(19)}
where $N_a$ counts all employed hours, including those in machine production, and $\rho V_mX$ is the return on the machine stock. If $r>0$, labor's share satisfies
\optdisp{\frac{wN_a}{I}\le\frac{(w/r)N_a}{(w/r)N_a+T},}{(20)}
with equality if $\rho=0$ and, when $N_a>0$, only if $\rho=0$. When $\rho=0$, at given $N_a$ and $T$, a lower wage--rent ratio lowers labor's share. With bounded total hours and fixed $T>0$, $w/r\to0$ implies that labor's share tends to zero.
\end{optthm}
Intermediate payments cancel when final expenditure is traced through the cost system, leaving the payments for all human hours and non-produced services and, from the machine-hour's row, the return on the stock (Appendix A).

When $\rho=0$, ownership of the non-produced input receives the non-wage component of income; with a positive return the owner also receives the return on machines, which (19) counts. The wage bill includes work throughout the network; eliminating labor from machine services leaves any human hours used elsewhere in the income identity. Interest, institutional rents, and transitional capacity rents add income components outside the flow benchmark. Labor's share responds jointly to the wage--rent ratio and employment, so increased employment can offset a local fall in $w/r$. When the conditions for (18) also hold, both the wage--rent ratio and the employed fraction tend to zero. In the data, the labor share's decline is documented and global (Karabarbounis and Neiman 2014), and its capital-side counterpart resolves substantially into housing, which is to say land (Rognlie 2015; Knoll, Schularick, and Steger 2017).

\optsec{6}{Distribution: rent taxation and a uniform transfer}
The income identity separates two sources of purchasing power: hours sold and ownership claims. A lower wage--rent ratio increases the importance of ownership at given employment. Rent taxation and transfers determine how this income is distributed across households.

An input is \emph{terminal over a horizon} when its supply cannot expand through production within that horizon. Land is a lasting example; installed grid connections or fabrication capacity may be terminal temporarily. Electricity, structures, and extraction are activities whose input costs are traced through the system. We reserve \emph{pure rent} for income due to fixed supply and \emph{site rent} for the land component of real-estate income, excluding structures. Several non-produced inputs enter as $\mathbf{B}\mathbf{r}$ in Appendix B.2. Long-run applications require persistent non-produced inputs.

\optsec{6.1}{The government}
The government has six instruments. It taxes the rental value of land at the rate $\tau_R\in[0,1]$, owed whether or not the land is used, as a land-value tax is; taxes wages at the rate $\tau_w\in[0,1)$; pays every worker a uniform transfer $d\ge0$; pays $g_w\ge0$ to a worker in work and $g_e\ge0$ to a worker in exit; and may provide the support basket itself ($\sigma=0$) in place of the owner ($\sigma=1$). A worker who sells $n$ hours then consumes $c$ baskets, and the owner $c_o$, with
\optdisp{\begin{aligned}
P_s\,c&=P_s+(1-\tau_w)wn+d+g_n,\qquad g_1=g_w,\quad g_0=g_e,\\
P_s\,(c_o+\sigma N)&=r\ell-\tau_RrT+\rho V_mX,
\end{aligned}}{(21)}
and the government's budget balances each period:
\optdisp{\tau_RrT+\tau_wwN_a=Nd+g_wN_a+g_e(N-N_a)+(1-\sigma)NP_s.}{(22)}
With a policy $(\tau_R,\tau_w,d,g_w,g_e,\sigma)$, Definition 1 changes in four places: each worker maximizes (5) subject to the first budget in (21), the owner's budget is the second in place of (7), the government's budget (22) holds, and the final good clears at $Y=N\int c(\chi)\,dF(\chi)+c_o$. Appendix A states the definition in full. A law that reserves tasks for people is a further policy, recorded in $H$ (Section 3.1).

Lemma 1, Propositions 1, 2, 4 and 5, and the first part of Corollary 2 hold as stated in every equilibrium with policy (Appendix A). Corollary 2's limit of participation also holds if no public payment favors work, $g_w\le g_e$; Appendix A shows how a payment that favors work can keep people working at any wage.

\optsec{6.2}{Sharing rents}
\begin{optthm}{Proposition 6}{sharing rents while labor remains employed}
Let the government tax the rental value of land, $R=rT$, at $\tau_R\in[0,1]$ and pay $d=\tau_RR/N$ to every worker, with $\tau_w=g_w=g_e=0$ and $\sigma=1$.
(i) At any positive rent the owner rents out all its land, whatever the tax rate. The physical supply of the fixed factor is invariant to a tax on its pure ownership rent. Producers continue to pay the gross competitive rental for using it.
(ii) An unconditional transfer pays the same amount in work and exit. Its direct payment differential at the participation boundary is zero, while participation can respond through income effects.
(iii) At given gross prices and hours, aggregate receipts remain $wN_a+R+\rho V_mX$, since $(1-\tau_R)R+Nd=R$. The tax redistributes the rent component toward an equal per-person allocation.
\end{optthm}
Part (i) holds because the tax does not depend on use (Lemma A.1(a)); parts (ii) and (iii) follow from the budgets (Appendix A).

\begin{optthm}{Corollary 3}{fiscal capacity as wage income vanishes}
(i) In the one-input economy, let $N,T>0$ be fixed and $N_a\le N$. Along a sequence of equilibria with $w/r\to0$, revenue from any wage tax with a rate between zero and one becomes a vanishing fraction of total income, while the non-wage share of income, $(R+\rho V_mX)/I$, tends to one; when $\rho=0$, $R/I\to1$. If the basket's total non-produced input requirement $B_s$ stays bounded away from zero, that revenue also funds a vanishing fraction of one basket per person.
(ii) At given methods that use no labor anywhere in the basket's chain, $\widetilde\lambda_s=0$, and any rent $r>0$, full rent capture yields coverage
\optdisp{\kappa=\frac{T}{N\widetilde b_s}.}{(24)}
It funds one basket per person exactly when $N\widetilde b_s\le T$, which implies $NB_s\le T$, the physical feasibility condition for the basket under these recipes; when $\rho=0$, $\widetilde b_s=B_s$ and the two conditions coincide.
\end{optthm}
Wage-tax revenue is at most $wN_a$, a vanishing share of (19) by Proposition 5; at methods with $\widetilde\lambda_s=0$, the cost system (4) gives $P_s=r\widetilde b_s$ (Appendix A). Part (ii) is a statement about prices at given methods; Appendix A says why such a configuration is not an equilibrium, states (24) with several non-produced inputs, and evaluates it with durable machines at the instance of Section 7.

Keynes (1930) looked forward to honoring ``the lilies of the field who toil not, neither do they spin.'' Equation (24) adds two conditions: the field must be large enough, and its rent must reach the lilies.

As the wage--rent ratio tends to zero, maintaining access to a living basket with a persistent non-produced input requirement increasingly depends on ownership income or transfers. The parents (Section 2) may themselves finance that support from wages. Support financed by another household member's wage faces the same aggregate constraint. A system funded entirely from wages becomes unable to sustain a fixed positive basket entitlement for everyone sufficiently far along the path, while labor may still be employed. A uniform rent dividend gives each person a claim on the income that remains. Broad ownership, collective funds, and other transfer arrangements can also distribute those claims. The financing constraint establishes the need for income beyond wages; institutions determine its distribution and the pace of adjustment.

Appendix A proves the corollary, and Appendices D.2 and D.3 develop payroll-tax incidence and rent-base coverage while labor remains employed. The fiscal lineage is George (1879); the Henry George theorem of Arnott and Stiglitz (1979) is related but distinct. Korinek and Lockwood (2026) show that in an economy without labor, taxing fixed factors is non-distortionary and can redistribute.
